WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.470
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday; I am hoooome!

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.080
Home is a nice place to be.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.279
I missed you, slightly off-white bookshelf.

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:07.590
And you, Dutch nerdfighter shoes.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.820
And I even missed you, probably mouse poop.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.610
So Hank I really liked your video about the
Renaissance and circumstance and as I've been

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.090
re-reading Behind the Beautiful Forevers for
the nerdfighter book club--by the way, nerdfighters,

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.140
don't forget to read this book by June 10th!--anyway,
I've been thinking a lot about luck and deserving

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.369
what happens to you, probably in part because
I feel especially undeserving (with good reason)

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:27.929
of all the nice things that are happening
to me right now.

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:30.619
Like Hank, people keep congratulating me after
they see The Fault in Our Stars movie as if

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:33.020
I did anything, but all I did was get lucky.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.790
Lucky that the book was published so well,
lucky to have a great cover, lucky that the

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.140
people I sold the movie rights to happened
to be brilliant, lucky that the wonderful

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.870
cast and crew and director came together to
make a great movie.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.150
I mean Hank I didn't make savvy decisions
in this process or anything; I just got lucky.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.870
There are much, much better books that have
been made into terrible, terrible movies.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.190
But Hank of course the luck goes further back
than that because I'm lucky to have you as

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.510
a brother and collaborator.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.620
I'm lucky that in 2007 you wrote a great song
about Harry Potter that gave us a whole new

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.620
audience.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.410
I happened to be born into a rich country
and I happened to be born male and white in

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.460
a culture that privileges male-ness and white-ness.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.789
And I write in English, Hank, which is a massive
advantage, I mean YA writers writing in Latvian,

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:13.520
even those far more talented than I am, rarely
find broad audiences.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.969
Like Hank, all this Fault in Our Stars movie
stuff is not happening because I am a good

00:01:16.969 --> 00:01:18.490
person; that's not the way the world works.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.369
And I hope I can remember that because when
you start to think that the good things that

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:24.640
have happened to you have happened because
you worked harder than other people, or because

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.929
the universe prefers you, you start to see
the world with this weird, self-centered myopia,

00:01:28.929 --> 00:01:32.790
and you don't commit yourself to lessening
inequality or to supporting marginalized voices

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.850
because you don't think there's a problem.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.079
You start to think that the world is as it
should be.

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:40.030
Now obviously, this is very bad for other
people, but I think ultimately it's also bad

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.030
for you.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.810
I don't mean you in particular, Hank, you're
a very nice person, I mean like the hypothetical

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.810
you.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.329
Because there will come a day when awful and
unjust things happen to you, and if you believe

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:51.619
that you deserved the good things because
you were oh-so-good, does that mean you now

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:53.350
deserve the bad things?

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.959
I would argue that in addition to not deserving
all of the wonderful things that happen in

00:01:56.959 --> 00:01:58.950
life, we don't deserve all of the terrible
things.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.229
Like Hank, you don't deserve to have ulcerative
colitis; that's not fair.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:05.789
No one deserves chronic pain or dementia or
poverty.

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:08.110
It's almost like "deserving" isn't the best
way to imagine it.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.869
So Hank I keep meaning to mention this, but
over at Crash Course, we're about to teach

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:13.370
a ten-episode series in Big History.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.860
This is possible because of a grant from one
of Bill Gates' organizations.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.410
Basically we get to chart the history of the
universe, from the Big Bang to, like, now.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.710
And I bring this up because in Big History,
we learn that 99.9% of matter in the Solar

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.189
System was sucked up by the Sun as it formed.

00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:33.370
Everything else: Jupiter, the Atlantic Ocean,
my astonishingly diverse collection of Polo

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.250
shirts, the probable mouse poop, all of that
is the .1%.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:43.330
In that context, everything in the solar system
not burning inside the Sun is on the very

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.370
far edge of the "good fortune" bell curve.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:51.090
To me at least, Hank, it's not about deserving
anything, because you are not your luck.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.370
No matter your circumstances, you are valuable
and rare and worthy of love.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.480
And the great thing about humans, my favorite
thing about humans, actually, is that we can

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.019
work together to improve each other's chances
of having healthy and productive lives.

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:07.040
And whether it's cancer research or the Project
for Awesome or being kind to a stranger at

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.459
Walmart, for me, that is the only real path
to gratitude.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:13.500
And I know that I'm saying this from an extremely
privileged position, but I really believe

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.170
that gratitude IS the proper response for
the absolute astonishment of getting to be

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:23.430
alive, and aware, and an essential part of
this crazy, sprawling story.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.140
In short, Hank, I am very grateful to you
and to all of nerdfighteria.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.260
Hey, I wanna tell you about two books I really
like.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.989
I know that you're busy reading Behind the
Beautiful Forevers, but!

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:34.469
Everything Leads to You by Nina LaCour and
The Great Greene Heist by Varian Johnson,

00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:35.530
both excellent!

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.030
Now I'm gonna try to go track down that mouse.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.139
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

